title: Cyclic Tasks
files:
  - em.prog:em.examples.basic/Ex13_TickerP.em.ts
  - em.core:em.utils/TickerMgr.em.ts
  - .assets/img-101-07-01.png

steps:
  - cmds:
      - view 3
    text: |

      {[ht,$start]&thinsp; Cyclic Tasks}

      This tour examines the {[cu]TickerP} program, which winks `AppLed` [1.0&thinsp;s] &thinsp;_and_ `SysLed` [1.5&thinsp;s] at different rates.

      Looking at the {[cu]TickerP} signal capture, aligned wakeups cause each wink to start at precise intervals from reset.

      In cases where these intervals coincide (such as the +3&thinsp;s mark), `AppLed`{[dc,A]} and `SysLed`{[dc,S]} wink in succession.

      This tour also introduces {[cu]TickerMgr} &ndash; yet another in&shy;stance of the **EMS** _object-factory_ design pattern.

  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 10
      - mark 1 B 13
      - mark 1 C 18
    focus: [1, 1]
    text: |

      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]TickerP% &ndash; Details}

      The {[cu]TickerP} program winks `SysLed` as well as `AppLed` to illustrate use of multiple {[cu]TickerMgr} objects.

      {[BM,A]} {[cu]TickerP} accesses each LED's {[cu]BoardC} {[cs]$delegate}.<br>
      {[BM,B]} {[cu]TickerP} declares a {[cs]$config} param for each object.<br>
      {[BM,C]} {[cu]TickerP} binds [**`meta`**] a pair of {[cu]TickerMgr} objects.

      Every {[cu]TickerMgr}.{[ct]Obj} in fact holds references to simi&shy;larly created [**`meta`**] {[cu]AlarmMgr} and {[cu]FiberMgr} objects.

  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 10
      - mark 1 B 13
      - mark 1 C 18
      - mark 1 D 28
    focus: [1, 27]
    text: |

      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]TickerP% &ndash; More Details}

      Within its {[cs]em$run} intrinsic, the {[cu]TickerP} program calls the {[cf]start} method on each {[cu]TickerMgr} object.

      Arguments to {[cf]start} not only specify the ticker's fre&shy;quency, but also designate a {[cs]$cb} invoked each cycle.

      {[in]While these bindings could have occurred at build-time, %[cf]start%'s flexibility has very little run-time cost.}

      The {[cf]appTickCb} and {[cf]sysTickCb} should look quite famil&shy;iar by now; use of the {[cu]Common}.{[cn]Mcu} proxy {[bm,D]} not so.

      When awakening from deep-sleep, some MCUs may trigger a _warm restart_ &ndash; ultimately reaching {[cs]em$run}.

      {[cu]TickerP} tests {[cn]Mcu}.{[cf]isWarm}{[bm,D]} to discriminate a _cold reset_ &ndash; ensuring we don't call {[cf]start} on every wakeup.

  - cmds:
      - open 2
      - mark 2 A 11
      - mark 2 B 17
      - mark 2 C 22
      - mark 2 D 26
      - mark 2 E 27
      - mark 2 F 28
    focus: [2, 1]
    text: |

      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]TickerMgr% &ndash; Details}

      {[BM,A]} The internal representation of a {[ct]Ticker} contains ref&shy;erences to {[cu]AlarmMgr} and {[cu]FiberMgr} objects.

      {[BM,B]} The client-visible API of {[ct]Ticker} objects includes the {[cf]start} method used in our {[cu]TickerP} program.

      {[BM,C]} An **EMS** {[cs]$table} (a key element of the _object-factory_ pattern) has traits similar to a {[cs]$config} param.

      {[BM,D]} The [**`meta`**] {[cn]ticker_tab}.**`$$add`** method  expands the underlying {[cs]$table} and returns a {[ct]Ticker} reference.

      {[BM,E]} {[cu]FiberMgr}.{[cf]create} receives the _index_ of our new {[ct]Ticker} as the arg value passed to this fiber's body.

      {[BM,F]} {[cu]AlarmMgr}.{[cf]create} connects {[cn]fiber} to {[cn]alarm} &ndash; with both references then retained in our new {[ct]Ticker}.

  - cmds:
      - open 2
      - mark 2 A 11
      - mark 2 B 17
      - mark 2 C 22
      - mark 2 D 26
      - mark 2 E 27
      - mark 2 F 28
      - mark 2 G 38
      - mark 2 H 39
      - mark 2 I 40
      - mark 2 J 41

    focus: [2, 26]
    text: |

      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]TickerMgr% &ndash; More Details}

      The {[cf]start} and {[cf]stop} methods will update {[ct]Ticker} fields before calling the appropriate {[cu]AlarmMgr} function.

      {[cf]alarmFB} lies at the heart of {[cu]TickerMgr} &ndash; implementing the body of the {[ct]Fiber}{[bm,E]} bound to our {[ct]Alarm}{[bm,F]}.

      {[BM,G]} We locate the {[ct]Ticker} object using its index {[bm,E]}.<br>
      {[BM,H]} We test for a {[cs]$null} callback (set inside {[cf]stop}).<br>
      {[BM,I]} We invoke the callback function at this point.<br>
      {[BM,J]} We schedule the next wakeup at the current rate.

  - cmds:
      - open 2
    focus: [2, 26]
    text: |

      {[ht,$extra]&thinsp;Extra Credit}

      {[bi,task_alt]} Add a {[cd]%%a} pulse to {[cf]alarmFB} and re-run {[cu]TickerP}.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Do {[ct]Ticker} objects really need to retain a {[ct]Fiber}.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Call {[cf]stop} and then invoke {[cf]start} with new args.

  - cmds:
      - open 2
    focus: [2, 1]
    text: |

      {[ht,$steps]&thinsp;Next Steps}

      The {[cu]TickerMgr} reinforces the **EMS** _object-factory_ de&shy;sign pattern, and shows how factories use one another.

      With {[cu]TickerMgr} and {[cu]FiberMgr} under our belt, the next tours covers the (complex) {[cu]AlarmMgr} implementation.

      {[in]We'll finally see %[cs]'@em.utils/TimeTypes.em'%&ndash; aka %[cu]T%.}

      {[hc]}
